{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The dashboard's "one slow endpoint" was two fast ones, waiting in line</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A dashboard takes 1.6 seconds to load its header. Someone opens the network tab expecting to find one slow request and instead finds two, each around 700-900ms, that never overlap — the second one doesn't even start until the first one finishes. The code reads <code>const user = await fetchUser(); const settings = await fetchSettings();</code>, written in the order a reasonable person would write it, top to bottom. Nothing about the code looks wrong. It just isn't fast.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>fetchSettings()</code> doesn't need anything from <code>fetchUser()</code>'s result. There was never a reason for the second request to wait for the first one, other than that <code>await</code> on line one makes the function pause there before line two ever executes.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>await</code> only pauses the <em>function</em> it's written in, not the two operations relative to each other. Code that reads top-to-bottom looks like it executes top-to-bottom, and for a function with dependent steps, it does. For two independent calls sitting on consecutive lines, that visual read is misleading: nothing forced them into that order except where the <code>await</code> keywords happened to land.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is a readability property working against a performance property. <code>async/await</code> was specifically designed to make asynchronous code <em>look</em> synchronous, so it reads as intent, do this, then this. That's a genuine win for code with real dependencies. Applied by habit to two calls with no dependency between them, the same visual cue that makes dependent code readable makes independent code silently slower, and nothing in the syntax marks the difference.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>One question decides sequential vs. concurrent: does this line need the last one's result?</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      <code>await fetchUser(); await fetchSettings();</code> starts <code>fetchUser</code>, waits for it to fully finish, and only then starts <code>fetchSettings</code>. Total time is roughly the first call's duration plus the second's. <code>Promise.all([fetchUser(), fetchSettings()])</code> starts both calls in the same tick, then waits for whichever one finishes last. Total time is roughly whichever call was slower, not the sum of both.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The fix isn't "always use <code>Promise.all</code>" — it's asking one question per <code>await</code>: does this line need a value produced by the line before it? If yes, sequential is correct and necessary, you have nothing to run concurrently yet. If no, calling both functions first (which starts both promises immediately) and awaiting the combined result afterward lets their underlying work, usually network I/O, overlap instead of queue.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      "Concurrent" isn't "parallel" here, and the distinction matters for reasoning about this correctly. JavaScript still runs one line of your code at a time on one thread. What overlaps when you call both functions before awaiting either is the <em>waiting</em>, both requests are in flight on the network simultaneously, even though the JS engine itself never executes two of your statements at once. The speedup comes from no longer serializing I/O latency you never needed to serialize, not from literal parallel execution of your code.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>Nobody wrote the slow version on purpose</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A checkout page fetched the user's saved shipping address and the cart's shipping-rate options as two separate calls to two separate services, written as consecutive <code>await</code> lines because that's the natural way to type "get the address, get the rates." Both calls existed for over a year before anyone profiled the page. The address call took about 650ms, the rates call about 850ms, and the two together took about 1.5 seconds, every time, because the rates call never started until the address call had fully returned.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      An engineer investigating unrelated checkout-abandonment numbers opened the network waterfall and noticed the two requests stacked instead of overlapping. Swapping the two sequential <code>await</code>s for a single <code>Promise.all</code> around both calls dropped the combined wait from about 1.5 seconds to about 850ms, roughly the slower of the two calls alone, with no change to either backend endpoint.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The team's actual follow-up was a lint pattern, not just this one fix: they started grepping for consecutive top-level <code>await</code> statements inside the same function with no data dependency between them, which turned up two more instances elsewhere in the codebase doing the same thing on other pages. The bug class was never really "this one endpoint is slow," it was "our code reads in an order that happens to match how well-written dependent async code looks," which is exactly the trap, and exactly why it's easy to reproduce by accident more than once.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

