{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The loop version of this trap, and where Promise.all is the wrong fix too</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The same mistake generalizes to loops: <code>for (const id of ids) { await fetchOne(id); }</code> awaits each fetch before starting the next one, serializing however many independent requests are in the list. <code>Promise.all(ids.map(id => fetchOne(id)))</code> starts them all at once instead.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>Promise.all</code> rejects the whole batch the instant any single promise rejects, which is fine for a checkout page's two required calls but wrong for a dashboard with several optional widgets, one flaky widget's fetch failing shouldn't blank the entire page. <code>Promise.allSettled</code> keeps the same concurrency win while letting you render whatever did succeed and handle each failure independently.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Firing every request in a large list simultaneously via <code>Promise.all(ids.map(...))</code> can trade one problem for another: a list of 200 IDs becomes 200 simultaneous requests, which can hit a browser's per-origin connection limit or a backend rate limit and get some of them queued or throttled anyway. For genuinely large independent batches, a bounded-concurrency pattern, running a fixed number at a time and starting the next as one finishes, gets the overlap win without the burst. The fix for this case's two-call trap is unconditional; the fix for an n-item loop needs that extra judgment call.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

