{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">Code that reads top-to-bottom with await looks sequential because that's how you read it, not because the two calls need to run in that order — check whether the second call actually needs the first one's result.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Ask one question per await: does this line need the value from the line before it? If not, start both calls first and await the combined result with Promise.all so their wait time overlaps instead of stacking.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Promise.all gives you concurrency, not parallelism — JS still runs one statement at a time, the win is overlapping I/O wait, not executing your code simultaneously. And Promise.all's fail-fast behavior and unbounded fan-out both need their own judgment calls for optional widgets and large batches.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
